# Undecidability

**Undecidability** is the absence of a method that settles a question. The
word is used for two different absences: a *sentence* that a given theory can
neither prove nor refute, and a *problem* that no algorithm solves for every
input. In this vault the card exists to take apart one proposed criterion for
a strange loop, that such a system is "irreducible in some sense". That
phrase hides three notions, of which undecidability is one.

## Description

### Where the card starts: "irreducible in some sense"

The fourth of the five properties proposed for a formal definition of a
strange loop read: the system is irreducible, it cannot be compressed to
rudimentary computations, but it can capture the patterns it is trying to
describe. The correction was that "irreducibility" is ambiguous, and that
Gödel's theorem concerns only one of its readings.

| Notion | What cannot be done | About | Card |
|---|---|---|---|
| Undecidability | settle the question at all, by proof or by algorithm | a sentence relative to a theory, or a problem | this one |
| Algorithmic incompressibility | describe an object by a program shorter than the object | one finite string | none yet |
| Computational irreducibility | predict the outcome faster than by running the computation | one computation | [[computational-irreducibility]] |

They come apart in both directions. A cellular automaton may be
computationally irreducible while every question about its state after $n$
steps is decidable: run it for $n$ steps. A random string is incompressible
and raises no decision problem at all. Gödel's theorem is about formal
incompleteness and says nothing general about the impossibility of
compression.

The essay on AI-oriented languages adds a fourth thing that is commonly
mistaken for the other three: a problem whose requirements are ambiguous or
incomplete. That is a missing specification, not a limit of computation.

### Two lanes inside the word

| | Undecidable sentence | Undecidable problem |
|---|---|---|
| Also called | independent of the theory | unsolvable, non-computable |
| Relative to | a particular theory $T$ | nothing: it is absolute |
| Means | $T$ proves neither $\sigma$ nor $\neg\sigma$ | no algorithm answers every instance correctly |
| Can be removed? | yes, by adding an axiom | no |
| Example | the [[godel-sentence]] of $T$; the continuum hypothesis over ZFC | the halting problem |

The second lane is the stronger one, and the first follows from it. If a
consistent, computably axiomatized theory extending Robinson arithmetic
decided every sentence of arithmetic, a program could answer any halting question by searching through
proofs. No program does that (Turing, 1936), so no such theory is complete.
This is a route to the [[incompleteness-theorems]] that does not mention a
self-referring sentence, though the unsolvability of the halting problem is
itself proved by [[diagonalization]].

The difference in the fourth row is what the discussion built on. Because a
sentence is undecidable only relative to its theory, somebody can decide it
from outside by adopting a new axiom. Then the larger theory has undecidable
sentences of its own. The sequence of theories produced this way is the
[[reflection-progression]]. Nothing comparable is available for the halting
problem: there is no larger notion of algorithm to move to.

The continuum hypothesis shows that undecidable sentences are not confined to
self-reference. Gödel's [[constructible-universe]] proves that ZFC cannot
refute it. Cohen's forcing (1963) proves that ZFC cannot prove it. No
diagonal sentence is involved.

### What this means for the definition of a strange loop

Irreducibility was dropped as a criterion. What replaced it in the Gödelian
definition is a *provable limitation*: a statement about the system, obtained
from its own self-reference, that the system demonstrably cannot settle. That
is undecidability in the first lane, produced by a fixed point. It is narrower
than "cannot be compressed" and it is something one can check.

It also bounds what any tool can be asked to do. A language model is a
computational system and does not escape the second lane. What it can help
with is the fourth thing above, turning an ambiguous requirement into a
specification, which is a different kind of difficulty.

## Relations

- It is not [[computational-irreducibility]] {relation: distinguished-from}: no shortcut to an answer, against no answer.
- Its sentence form is what the [[incompleteness-theorems]] {relation: generalizes} establish for arithmetic.
- Its standard example of an undecided sentence is the [[godel-sentence]] {relation: example-of}.
- Its problem form is proved by [[diagonalization]] {relation: uses}.
- It is what each stage of a [[reflection-progression]] {relation: uses} removes for one sentence and regains for another.
- The [[constructible-universe]] {relation: uses} supplies half of the independence of the continuum hypothesis.
- It is the provable limitation required of a Gödelian [[reflective-formal-system]] {relation: part-of}.
- It is distinct from [[tarski-undefinability]] {relation: distinguished-from}, a limit on defining truth and not on deciding.
- It bounds, without being the same as, the [[epistemic-limits]] {relation: analogous-to} conjectured for reflective agents.
- It bears on the question [[ai-oriented-language]] {relation: answers}: the line between programs and models is not the line between decidable and undecidable.

## Neighbouring concepts

- **[[computational-irreducibility]].** A computation whose outcome cannot be
  had substantially faster than by performing it. The answer exists and is
  reachable. It is only expensive.
- **[[incompleteness-theorems]].** Every consistent, effective, sufficiently
  strong theory leaves some arithmetical sentence undecided. It is
  undecidability of the first lane, guaranteed.
- **[[reflection-progression]].** Theories extended by the sentences they
  could not decide. It is what one does about an undecidable sentence, and
  why doing it never finishes.
- **[[godel-sentence]].** The sentence equivalent to its own unprovability.
  It is the undecidable sentence a theory writes about itself.

